<Doc
  title="Plans and limits"
  description="What each account plan allows, and what happens when a limit is reached."
  canonicalPath="/docs/plans/"
>
  <p>
    Every account is on a plan. Plans decide how many machines and projects you may keep, and how
    long the audit log is retained. There is no self-serve billing yet: the free plan is the default
    for everyone.
  </p>

  <h2>Free</h2>

  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th></th>
        <th>Live machines</th>
        <th>Projects</th>
        <th>Audit retention</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td><code>free</code></td>
        <td>10</td>
        <td>25</td>
        <td>90 days</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <p>
    The free caps are deliberately generous: they are there so the enforcement path is real, not to
    constrain ordinary personal use. They will tighten when billing lands.
  </p>

  <h2>Pro - coming soon</h2>

  <p>
    A paid plan with higher limits is on the way. Nothing is charged today, and there is no
    self-serve upgrade yet. When it ships, this page will list what it includes.
  </p>

  <h2>What counts</h2>

  <p>
    <strong>Machines</strong> count only while they are live. Revoking a machine from the dashboard
    frees its slot immediately; the row stays for the audit trail, but it no longer consumes the
    plan. Deleting a revoked machine is permanent and also frees nothing extra, because the slot was
    already free.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>Projects</strong> count every project on the account. Re-registering a project that
    already exists under the same slug (what <code>exeora connect</code> does on a machine that is
    already known) does not consume a new slot.
  </p>

  <h2>When a limit is hit</h2>

  <p>
    Registering one machine or project too many returns a clear error from the CLI and a{" "}
    <code>403</code> with <code>error: "plan_limit"</code> from the API. The dashboard Settings page
    shows the current plan and how much of each cap is in use.
  </p>

  <h2>Usage</h2>

  <p>
    Tool calls are rolled up once a night into a per-day counter that survives the audit-log prune.
    Settings shows the total for the current UTC month. The rollup is metering, not a billable meter:
    nothing is charged against it today.
  </p>
</Doc>
